capability java-properties/read-text  the keys and values a properties file's text writes, as `Properties.load(Reader)` reads it
  heroes  java_properties.parse, java_properties.Entry
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    none of the three standard libraries reads the format, so Java's `java.util.Properties` is the reference, its answers observed of OpenJDK 27.0.1.1 on 2026-11-22 for every text the tests hold: line ends LF, CR LF and CR alone; white space a space, a tab or a form feed; `#` and `!` comments, never continued; an odd count of trailing backslashes continuing a line, the next line's leading white space dropped and a `#` there read as content; the key ending at `=`, `:` or white space; the escapes `\t \n \r \f`, `\uXXXX` and a backslash before any other character. A key read twice keeps its first place and its last value, as Java's table keeps the last. Java answers "Malformed \uxxxx encoding." for a `\u` without four hexadecimal digits, refused here `invalid` with its line; Java keeps a lone half of a surrogate pair in its UTF-16 string, which no text of UTF-8 holds, so it is refused `invalid` too, and two `\u` escapes of a pair join into one character. A byte order mark stays a character of the first key, as Java keeps it

