capability java-properties/write  entries written as a properties file that reads back, as `Properties.store(Writer)` writes them
  heroes  java_properties.format, java_properties.format_with, java_properties.WriteOptions, java_properties.default_write_options
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Java's lines observed byte for byte on 2026-11-22: every space of a key and the first of a value behind a backslash; tab, line feed, carriage return and form feed as `\t \n \r \f`; `=`, `:`, `#`, `!` and `\` behind a backslash; other controls written as they are. The entries are written in their own order, where Java 18 and later sort the keys: sort the entries first for Java's file. A key twice is refused `invalid`, since the file would read back as one. Lines end in a line feed on every platform, where Java writes the platform's own

capability java-properties/write-ascii  entries written as ASCII alone, as `Properties.store(OutputStream)` writes them
  heroes  java_properties.ascii_write_options
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    every character outside space to `~` as `\uXXXX` with upper-case digits, a character past U+FFFF as the two halves of its surrogate pair, as Java wrote "é€😀" for "é€😀"; the file reads alike as UTF-8 and as ISO 8859-1, so `decode` and `decode_latin1` both read it back

capability java-properties/comments  comment lines written before the entries
  heroes  java_properties.WriteOptions (comments)
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Java's `store(writer, comments)`: each line end of the comments begins a line behind a `#`, unless the line opens with `#` or `!` already, as Java wrote "first line\nsecond\r\n#third\n!fourth" on 2026-11-22; to a stream Java writes a comment's characters up to U+00FF as bytes of ISO 8859-1, written here as `\uXXXX` like every other, so the file stays ASCII; a reader drops a comment either way. An empty comment, which Java writes as a `#` alone, writes no line here

